ltiple_choice","prompt":"For a normal distribution, approximately what proportion lies within two standard deviations of the mean?","answer_data":{"answer":"0.95"},"explanation":"The empirical rule gives about 95% within two standard deviations.","difficulty":"foundation","question_style":"recall","calculator":"not_allowed","estimated_time_seconds":35,"marks_estimate":1,"tags":["normal distribution","recall"],"choices":["0.50","0.68","0.95","0.997"],"correct_choices":["0.95"]},{"seed_id":"51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000149","syllabus_code":"SL 4.9","type":"numeric_answer","prompt":"For X~N(70,8²), find the z-score corresponding to X=86.","answer_data":{"answer":2,"tolerance":0},"explanation":"z=(86−70)/8=2.","difficulty":"standard","question_style":"procedural","calculator":"not_allowed","estimated_time_seconds":60,"marks_estimate":2,"tags":["normal distribution","standardization","procedural"],"choices":[],"correct_choices":[2]},{"seed_id":"51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000150","syllabus_code":"SL 4.9","type":"true_false","prompt":"A normal distribution is symmetric, so its mean and median are equal.","answer_data":{"answer":true},"explanation":"Symmetry about the centre makes the mean and median coincide.","difficulty":"extension","question_style":"conceptual","calculator":"neutral","estimated_time_seconds":75,"marks_estimate":2,"tags":["normal distribution","symmetry","conceptual"],"choices":["True","False"],"correct_choices":["True"]},{"seed_id":"51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000151","syllabus_code":"SL 4.10","type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"When is Spearman’s rank correlation generally more suitable than Pearson’s correlation?","answer_data":{"answer":"When the association is monotonic but not necessarily linear"},"explanation":"Spearman uses ranks and measures monotonic association.","difficulty":"foundation","question_style":"recall","calculator":"not_allowed","estimated_time_seconds":35,"marks_estimate":1,"tags":["Spearman rank","method selection","recall"],"choices":["When the association is monotonic but not necessarily linear","Only when both variables are categorical","When there are no paired observations","Only when the sample size is exactly 10"],"correct_choices":["When the association is monotonic but not necessarily linear"]},{"seed_id":"51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000152","syllabus_code":"SL 4.10","type":"numeric_answer","prompt":"Six paired observations have Σd²=14. Find Spearman’s rank coefficient using rₛ=1−6Σd²/[n(n²−1)], correct to three decimals.","answer_data":{"answer":0.6,"tolerance":0.001},"explanation":"rₛ=1−84/[6(35)]=1−0.4=0.6.","difficulty":"standard","question_style":"procedural","calculator":"allowed","estimated_time_seconds":60,"marks_estimate":2,"tags":["Spearman rank","calculation","procedural"],"choices":[],"correct_choices":[0.6]},{"seed_id":"51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000153","syllabus_code":"SL 4.10","type":"multi_select","prompt":"Select all true statements about Spearman’s rank coefficient.","answer_data":{"answers":["It is based on ranks.","Values near −1 indicate strong negative monotonic association.","It is usually less sensitive to extreme raw values than Pearson’s r."]},"explanation":"Ranking reduces the impact of raw magnitudes, but no correlation coefficient proves causation.","difficulty":"extension","question_style":"conceptual","calculator":"neutral","estimated_time_seconds":75,"marks_estimate":2,"tags":["Spearman rank","interpretation","conceptual"],"choices":["It is based on ranks.","Values near −1 indicate strong negative monotonic association.","It is usually less sensitive to extreme raw values than Pearson’s r.","It proves causation when |rₛ| is large."],"correct_choices":["It is based on ranks.","Values near −1 indicate strong negative monotonic association.","It is usually less sensitive to extreme raw values than Pearson’s r."]},{"seed_id":"51000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000154","syllabus_code":"SL 4.11","type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"A test uses signi